 abstract = {CONTEXT
Climate change poses unprecedented threats to Globally Important Agricultural Heritage Systems (GIAHS) and ancient water management networks worldwide. Sites such as the Ifugao Rice Terraces, Persian qanats, Andean potato fields, and Ethiopian coffee landscapes, which embody centuries of traditional ecological knowledge and cultural identity, are increasingly destabilized by rising temperatures, erratic precipitation, and extreme weather events. Their degradation risks not only the loss of cultural heritage but also undermines food security, water sustainability, and climate resilience for dependent communities.
OBJECTIVE
This study aims to: (1) assess the multifaceted vulnerability of agricultural and water heritage sites to climate impacts; (2) document and evaluate traditional adaptation strategies and their integration with modern resilience technologies; and (3) analyze existing policy frameworks to propose actionable, culturally sensitive conservation pathways.
METHODS
We synthesize global case studies using a mixed-methods approach, integrating peer-reviewed literature, IPCC climate data, satellite remote sensing analyses, and policy reviews of UNESCO’s Climate Change Initiative and FAO’s GIAHS program. The research examines technological interventions, including AI-driven modeling and IoT sensors alongside community-based adaptation practices through a comparative analytical framework spanning diverse heritage systems from East Asia to the Mediterranean.
RESULTS AND CONCLUSIONS
Key findings reveal severe, site-specific vulnerabilities: 20–30% yield declines in heritage rice terraces; 15–25% reductions in Andean potato production; 38% of active Iranian qanats dried up between 2005–2020; and accelerated pest proliferation due to warming. Successful adaptation emerges when Indigenous knowledge is integrated with modern tools, for example, IoT-enhanced Balinese subak systems achieved 35% water savings, and Andean frost prediction apps improved irrigation efficiency by 40%. However, only 23% of heritage sites have completed climate vulnerability assessments, and 78% lack computational infrastructure for real-time monitoring. Policy gaps, funding shortages, and weak governance hinder large-scale implementation.
SIGNIFICANCE
Agricultural heritage sites are living laboratories for climate adaptation, offering time-tested models for biodiversity conservation, resource efficiency, and community-based management. Their preservation is critical not only for safeguarding cultural identity and agrobiodiversity but also for informing global food and water security strategies. This study underscores that effective conservation requires interdisciplinary collaboration, equitable resource allocation, and respectful integration of traditional and scientific knowledge systems. Without urgent, culturally sensitive action, these irreplaceable systems risk irreversible decline within 30–50 years, with cascading impacts on local livelihoods and global sustainability.},
